{{!-- Membership: the Surface that sells.

Shared by page-membership.hbs, custom-membership.hbs and the two Layout override templates. The
Layout arrives as `layout`, read as `../layout` inside `{{#post}}` — the same argument About takes,
from the same `@custom.page_hero` — and it decides the opener and nothing else.

THE PRICES ARE THE PUBLICATION'S, NEVER THE FIXTURE'S — see membership/tiers.hbs, which is the
whole reason this Surface exists in the port at all.

WHAT THE DESIGN DRAWS THAT GHOST HAS NO FIELD FOR. The comparison matrix, the member quotes and
the FAQ are all editor content here rather than theme markup, and each for its own reason:

  - The matrix is benefit × tier. Ghost stores `benefits` as a list PER TIER and Handlebars can
    neither union three lists nor test membership in one, so the matrix cannot be derived from
    what the platform holds. A publication writes it as a table and the theme styles it —
    `.mirror-content table` in assets/css/screen.css — inside a scroll container, so the tier in
    the last column is reachable at every width rather than cropped off the page.
  - The quotes are testimonials. There is no member-quote resource, and a theme that invented
    three would be putting words in a reader's mouth.
  - The FAQ is a Koenig toggle card, which is Ghost's own accordion: real markup, the platform's
    JS, and something a buyer can edit. `card_assets` keeps toggle's assets for exactly this.

The order follows the rule the About page sets: prose the publication wrote is one `{{content}}`
block, and the sections the theme derives bracket it rather than interrupting it. The design
interleaves them; a Ghost page has one body. Recorded as a Concession in DECISIONS.md. --}}
{{#post}}
	{{#match ../layout "Split hero"}}
		{{> "membership/hero-split" eyebrow=(t "Membership")}}
	{{else}}
		{{> "centered-intro" eyebrow=(t "Membership") headingId="mem-h" padding="pt-22 pb-14" titleSpacing="mt-6" titleClass="max-w-[20ch] text-[clamp(36px,5.2vw,64px)]" descriptionClass="text-lead mt-[22px] max-w-[52ch] leading-[1.55]"}}
	{{/match}}

	{{#match ../layout "Split hero"}}
		{{> "membership/tiers" padding="pt-14"}}
	{{else}}
		{{> "membership/tiers"}}
	{{/match}}

	{{#if html}}
		<section
			aria-label="{{t "What's included"}}"
			style="--container-max:1120px"
			class="container pt-20">
			{{!-- THE MATRIX MUST NEVER CROP ITS LAST COLUMN, which is the tier being sold. A table
			stops shrinking at its min-content width — four columns of benefit labels is roughly
			400px — so below the stacking width it pushes the page wider than the viewport and takes
			the rightmost tier off the edge. The reference build swaps the table for labelled cards
			there; a theme cannot restructure HTML it did not write, so the block scrolls instead
			and every column stays reachable. `overflow-x` makes `overflow-y` compute to `auto`, so
			the 4px of block padding is what keeps a focus ring inside a toggle card from being
			clipped — the same trick the featured strip uses, and the negative margin gives the
			space back. --}}
			<div
				data-scroll-intent="benefit matrix, reachable instead of cropped"
				class="mirror-content -my-1 overflow-x-auto py-1">{{content}}</div>
		</section>
	{{/if}}

	{{> "membership/locked"}}

	{{> "newsletter" anchor="membership-newsletter" pt="pt-20"}}
{{/post}}
